## User preferences

- when the user asked "Can you watch the videos that have already been generated? So that you can know my style" -> future BMH course generation should inspect approved cuts and frame sheets before generating new stills or animations [Task 1]
- when the user was frustrated about wasted work and wrong lesson targeting -> verify the exact lesson, slot, and cue range before editing or generating anything for course production [Task 1]
- when the user asked for "the exact prompts... models, etc. etc." and said "Pretend like you are a senior engineer providing directions to another agent/llm" -> answer exact-replication requests with a copy-paste handoff that includes refs, model choices, prompt fragments, and guardrails rather than vague advice [Task 2]

## Reusable knowledge

- `course-assets/review-lesson4B/LESSON-4B-v1-APPROVED.mp4` is the preserved style anchor for the approved 4B cut, and the authoritative lesson state lives in `module-04-lesson4B-still-review.md`, `module-04-lesson4B-animation-prompts.md`, `module-04-lesson4B-animation-production-plan.md`, `module-04-lesson4B-current-gate.md`, and `module-04-lesson4B-assembly-review.md` [Task 1]
- The reviewed 4B working asset roots are `course-assets/scenes/module-04-lesson4B/` and `course-assets/heygen/lesson4B/`; prefer these lesson-scoped paths over broad session-log greps when reconstructing the final recipe [Task 1]
- The final still-generation recipe used the built-in image workflow with `gpt-image-2` plus attached refs `docs/design/style-ref-1.png`, `docs/design/style-ref-2.png`, and `docs/design/cast-board.png`; exact prompt fragments that mattered included flat sticker-sheet doodle style, thick wobble black outlines, tiny dot eyes, tiny centered two-stroke/comma nose, cornflower-blue background, no text, no gradients, no shadows, no perspective, and no ambient doodles [Task 2]
- For 4B animation, `seedance_2_0` was the accepted model for B2/B3/B4A/B4B/B5/B6/B7/B8, while `kling3_0` was tested and rejected for B4B because the mouth motion was too frantic [Task 2]
- Prompts alone were not enough to reproduce the approved output; the working recipe also depended on the attached refs, character locks, palette flattening, and visual approval/finalizer checks [Task 1][Task 2]
- Andrea and other narrator delivery should stay at HeyGen voice speed `1.0` / default speed; any guide, template, scene card, or script that still says `0.95` is stale and should be corrected before regeneration [Task 3][ad-hoc note]
- When an AI animation clip ends before the beat or transition ends, hold that clip's own extracted final frame; do not fall back to the source still and do not use forward/reverse loops [Task 4][ad-hoc note]

## Failures and how to do differently

- symptom: prompt extraction turns noisy and unreliable; cause: broad searches across session logs surface unrelated artifacts; fix: narrow to lesson-scoped docs, reviewed assets, final manifests, and current-gate/assembly-review files first [Task 1][Task 2]
- symptom: a "replicate exactly" handoff still fails to reproduce the approved look; cause: only prompt text was handed off; fix: include refs, model choice, rejected-model reason, negative constraints, and postprocess/finalizer requirements together [Task 2]
- symptom: regenerated course narration sounds oddly slow; cause: stale templates still say Andrea speed `0.95`; fix: reset to HeyGen/default speed `1.0` unless Jarrad explicitly asks for a different delivery [Task 3][ad-hoc note]
- symptom: an animation ends early and the tail looks wrong; cause: the assembly falls back to a source still or loop trick; fix: hold the clip's own extracted final frame through the remaining beat/transition duration [Task 4][ad-hoc note]

